% What this device reads where it holds no pixel, for three pages
% cleared to three different colours. Run against a live device
% (-d <dev>) by run-device-ground-test.sh, which reads the answers of
% the whole roster and holds them against each other.
%
% This file asserts nothing. The question is whether the devices agree,
% and no single run of a single device can see that: a device asked what
% its own ground is will always say what it says. So each run reports
% its three readings and the wrapper does the comparing.
%
% erasepage clears the page to grey 1.0 (PLRM 8.2), so the colour a page
% is cleared to is not an operand of it. What that grey comes to is the
% transfer function's answer, which is how three different pages are had
% here.

/dev .privatedict /.graphicsdict get /currgstate get /device get def
/getpix { dev dup /GetPix get exec } def

% Read a pixel, taking what came back rather than assuming it. How many
% components a device reports is the device's own business, and a read
% that refuses reports none; either way the stack is left at the depth
% the read was handed.
/readpix { % x y  .  [c1..cN]
    count 2 sub /rp.depth exch def
    { getpix } stopped {
        count rp.depth sub dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat }{ pop } ifelse
        [ ]
    }{
        count rp.depth sub array astore
    } ifelse
} def

% The reading is taken off the page, where there is nothing to hold a
% pixel and a mark aimed there is dropped, so what a read answers is the
% ground and nothing else. Every device answers there, whether or not it
% can report a pixel it holds.
/report { % (tag) proc  .  -
    gsave settransfer erasepage grestore
    -1 -1 readpix
    exch print
    { ( ) print 20 string cvs print } forall
    (\n) print
} def

(GROUND full:) { } report
(GROUND high:) { pop 0.75 } report
(GROUND low:)  { pop 0.25 } report
